# Target C: maximum population forces six first curvature profiles to match

Date: 2026-10-07. Authority: audit.77.
**Proved candidate awaiting independent audit.** No promotion.

## 1. A general boundary-family theorem

Fix M>=3. Let W(theta), theta>=0, be a C2 family of convex unit polygons
with 6M labelled vertices, tending to the regular radius-M triangular
hexagon H_M. Divide its cyclic boundary into six sides with M edges each.
At theta=0, the directed edges on side k are e_(k+2), where
`e_k=exp(i*k*pi/3)`. Write their first angular rates as

`edge_(k,j)(theta)=e_(k+2)*exp(i*beta_(k,j)*theta+O(theta^2))`,
`j=0,...,M-1`.

Assume beta_(k,0)<...<beta_(k,M-1) on each side; no symmetry, common
profile, corner motion, or exact sorting wall is assumed. Suppose an
arbitrary unit-separated cloud of N=3M^2-3M+1 points can be placed in
the strict interior for arbitrarily small positive theta, jointly
unit-separated from all wall vertices.

Then the six ordered lists beta_k are identical. After removing one
first-order translation and rotation, their common mean is zero. Every
subsequential first cloud velocity is a sorting-permutation field for
this common rate multiset, even though the boundary family itself need
not be an exact finite sorting wall.

Thus if any two lists differ, there is theta_0>0 such that the wall
cannot accommodate N interior points for 0<theta<theta_0. Oler and
continuity bound the population by N, so the maximum is at most N-1.
This is an actual finite-population exclusion, not just nonexistence of
a C2 cloud trajectory. The cloud need not depend continuously on theta.

`arbitrary six first boundary profiles -> incompatible maximum population
unless they match -> one-deficit finite population -> packet using actual
boundary data (still required)`.

The last packet is not proved for arbitrary beta_k. Matching first
profiles do not identify the finite wall or justify importing the fixed
sorting-wall packet. No arbitrary macroscopic-container extraction,
uniform growing-M onset, C-II or Level I closure follows.

## 2. Full chart and first velocities

Oler equality at the limiting H_M forces the complete triangular chart
P_M; its wall consists of the given 6M boundary labels and its interior
is P_(M-1). This is the equilateral finite equality implication at the
normalization independently checked in audit.74, not a disk-equality
uniqueness assertion. The reasoning applies to this different boundary
family because only its limiting H_M and full equality count are used.

Label a feasible subsequence by that limit. Cloud displacement is O(theta):
otherwise divide by its norm, let the O(theta) wall displacement disappear,
and obtain a nonzero flat pinned lower-cone vector. Weight one on every
unit edge with a moving endpoint equilibrates at every full-chart
interior point. Positivity forces all unit derivatives to be zero; the
connected elementary triangles propagate pinning and force the vector
zero. This is an auxiliary-chart argument, not full-source ZR/ZF.
Consequently a subsequence has displacement theta*d+o(theta).
Let g be the prescribed first wall velocities.

Each flat unit wall edge has zero first length variation. Sum those M
rows along a side, then sum over the six sides. The telescoping identity
`e_(k+1)-e_(k+2)=e_k` gives

`sum_k e_k dot g(M*e_k)=0`.

For each r=1,...,M-1 use the flat positive stress of weight one on ring r
and the six radial chains from that ring to the wall corners. For r=0
use the six full radial chains alone. Its interior forces balance and
its wall work is exactly the preceding sum. Every lower first row is
nonnegative, so each ring and radial row in these stresses is zero.
These stresses are used individually, not summed as energy currency.

Radial equalities give
`d(r*e_k)=d(0)+alpha_(r,k)*J(e_k)`.
Ring side equalities imply alpha_(r,k) is independent of k. At r=M,
use the actual unit-wall edge equalities summed along each straight
limiting side to obtain the same conclusion. Hence

`g(M*e_k)=d(0)+alpha_M*J(e_k)`.

Subtract translation theta*d(0) and rotation of first rate alpha_M/M
from the entire family. Now the corner first velocities vanish and
all six beta_k have zero sum. Indeed their side sums were alpha_M
before normalization. Thus unequal side means already obstruct a
feasible maximum population, before the second-order argument.

## 3. The projected first cone is an intersection of permutahedra

Put alpha_0=alpha_M=0, t_h=alpha_(h+1)-alpha_h, sum t_h=0.
In sector k use rotated coordinates u=e_k, v=e_(k+1), c=sqrt(3)/2.
At p=(r-j)u+jv, ring equality gives

`d(p) dot u=c*s_(r,j)`,
`d(p) dot v=c*(s_(r,j)+alpha_r)`.

The remaining two nearest-neighbour lower rows give

`s_(r,j)>=s_(r-1,j)`,
`s_(r,j)>=s_(r-1,j-1)-t_(r-1)`.

Axis values are s_(r,0)=0 and s_(r,r)=-alpha_r. On wall side k the
first velocity at index j is J(e_(k+2))*B_(k,j), where
`B_(k,j)=sum_(ell<j) beta_(k,ell)`; hence s_(M,j)=-B_(k,j).

Following any choice of j diagonal steps to this endpoint gives

`sum_(h in I) t_h >= B_(k,j)` for every j-element subset I.

Since beta_k is ordered increasingly with sum zero, these inequalities
say exactly

`t in P_k=conv{permutations of beta_k}`.

The projected feasible first polytope is the intersection of all six
P_k. For sufficiency in this first-order statement, at interior layers
choose s_(r,j) as minus the sum of the j smallest t's in the prefix;
at layer M use the specified -B_(k,j) independently for each sector.
The subset bounds give each entering inequality, and axes agree.
No finite motion is inferred from this weak first-order sufficiency.

## 4. A single finite stress inequality forbids profile mismatch

Let x_k(theta) be the actual corner positions, after the normalization
above. Sum the projections of all unit wall edges onto their flat side
directions. Telescoping, and expanding the cosines, gives the exact
second-order corner work

`sum_k e_k dot (x_k(theta)-M*e_k)`
`=-(theta^2/2)*sum_k ||beta_k||^2+o(theta^2)`.

Use only the central six-radial-chain stress in the finite lower squared
inequalities. All interior linear terms cancel. Their quadratic terms
therefore satisfy

`sum_(radial chains) |delta_p-delta_q|^2`
`>=-2*sum_k e_k dot (x_k(theta)-M*e_k)`.

Divide by theta^2 along the arbitrary feasible subsequence. The left
limit is 6*||t||^2, so

`6*||t||^2 >= sum_k ||beta_k||^2`.

Conversely t in P_k implies ||t||^2<=||beta_k||^2 for each k.
Define L_k=||beta_k||^2-||t||^2>=0. The stress inequality gives
sum L_k<=0, so every L_k=0. Strict convexity of squared norm on a
convex combination of distinct permutations forces t to be a permutation
of each beta_k. Because each list is increasing, all six lists match.
At a permutation the prefix/suffix path equalities force every s_(r,j)
to its sorting value, as in the earlier fixed-profile argument. Thus the
entire first cloud field is classified.

For a quantitative mismatch certificate, for t in P_k write a convex
combination t=sum lambda_v*v of its permutations. Then

`L_k=sum lambda_v*|v-t|^2 >= dist(t,perm(beta_k))^2`.

For any two sides k,l, the closest pair of their permutation vectors
has squared distance
`d_kl^2=sum_j (beta_(k,j)-beta_(l,j))^2`, by rearrangement.
The triangle inequality and (sqrt(L_k)+sqrt(L_l))^2<=2(L_k+L_l)
give

`sum_s L_s >= L_k+L_l >= d_kl^2/2`.

Thus any mismatch gives a definite positive deficit against the required
zero, regardless of whether the two profiles have equal energies. This
is not a floating LP margin. The argument also covers real profiles.

## 5. Nonvacuous scope, certificates and the finite gap

Closed unequal-profile wall families exist. Choose three different strictly
increasing zero-sum lists for sides 0,1,2, and repeat them on opposite
sides 3,4,5. Opposite edge sums cancel exactly. At sufficiently small theta
all side turns are positive and the six corner turns stay near pi/3,
so this is a closed convex unit wall tending to H_M. The theorem excludes
its maximum interior population; it does not exclude the wall itself.

The exact checker records two opposed-profile constructions, including a
same-energy distinct-profile pair. It verifies means, energies and positive
rearrangement-distance certificates with rational arithmetic. Its counts
are finite examples. The arbitrary-cloud and sufficiently-small-theta
conclusion is the subsequence, force and convexity proof above.

This theorem transfers a *necessary first boundary profile* from maximal
packing to six genuinely different side families. It does not identify
higher wall coefficients, local finite sorting isolation under unmatched
higher coefficients, or a packet after the resulting deficit. Those are
separate exact obligations; no canonical status is changed.
